              "type": "reading_comp_select_passage",
              "passageText": "Behavioral economists examine cognitive heuristics and structural biases that systematically alter consumer decision-making. One pervasive phenomenon is anchoring bias, wherein individuals rely disproportionately on an initial piece of numerical information when making subsequent quantitative judgments, regardless of that information's objective relevance.\n\nIn retail environments, marketers strategically leverage anchoring by displaying elevated original prices alongside discounted sale prices. Even when consumers suspect that original list prices are artificially inflated, the initial numerical anchor anchors expectations of product value, significantly increasing purchase probability compared to unanchored pricing displays.",
