            "type": "reading_comp_single",
            "passageText": "Palaeoecological records extracted from lake sediment cores indicate significant structural shifts in temperate forest composition across the Northern Hemisphere following the terminal glacial retreat. Radiocarbon dating of fossilized pollen grains reveals that cold-tolerant spruce and birch species rapidly migrated northward as regional temperatures elevated, gradually giving way to oak, beech, and pine communities in temperate latitudes.\n\nThis ecological transition demonstrates the high sensitivity and dynamic responsiveness of plant species to post-glacial climatic forcing. Rather than migrating as unified, cohesive botanical communities, individual species responded independently to shifting temperature and moisture gradients, generating novel plant associations unprecedented in the modern ecological literature.",
