            "id": "gre91-v1-rc2",
            "type": "reading_comp_multiple",
            "passageText": "Literary scholars frequently debate whether European Romanticism marked an abrupt ideological break from Enlightenment rationalism or a continuation of foundational humanistic inquiry. Although Romantic essayists and poets explicitly rejected the mechanical determinism of Newtonian physics in favor of emotional intuition and individual subjective experience, their central idealization of personal moral autonomy drew heavily upon Enlightenment ethical philosophy.\n\nRather than abandoning philosophical rigor, Romantic thinkers repurposed Enlightenment concepts of freedom and universal human dignity to critique the alienating effects of early industrial capitalism, asserting that true intellectual freedom required nurturing imaginative capacity alongside analytical reason.",
