              "passageText": "The transition in observational astrophysics from steady-state cosmological models to the expanding universe model relied heavily on precise spectral observations of cosmic microwave background (CMB) radiation. First detected by Penzias and Wilson in 1965, this faint isotropic radiation field provides a thermal snapshot of the early universe approximately 380,000 years after the Big Bang, when photons decoupled from baryonic matter.\n\nHowever, early critics raised significant challenges to the expanding universe paradigm by advancing tired-light hypotheses. Advocates of tired-light mechanisms proposed that spectral redshifts resulted not from the recessional velocity of distant galaxies, but rather from photons losing energy through interactions with interstellar dust and intergalactic plasma over vast distances. Subsequent observational data from space-based instruments such as the COBE and WMAP satellites dismantled the tired-light framework by demonstrating that the CMB spectrum matches a perfect blackbody radiation curve to within one part in ten thousand—a signature that tired-light scattering models could not account for without introducing severe spectral distortions.",
